WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:03.247
- Wow, that's a deep question.

00:00:03.247 --> 00:00:06.660
(gentle music)

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:09.510
It's funny, it's actually like in Spanish,

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:11.550
but the word is like inutil,

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.490
and basically what that means
is like good for nothing.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:18.720
And yeah, it's just like
it's just a mean thing

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.420
to tell somebody 'cause
everybody's good at something.

00:00:22.721 --> 00:00:25.353
And yeah, I don't believe
that anymore, but yeah.

00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.380
It was my mom and she
(beep) hurt my feelings

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.750
saying that (beep) but
I don't know how deep

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:32.340
you want this to be.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:34.950
She's different now and she
like apologized for that.

00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:38.400
But yeah, like she said
that to me once or twice

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:39.450
or many times growing up

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:40.770
and I remember I just hearing that word

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.720
and it would always just like
hurt me in my soul and yeah.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.250
Words definitely have power.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:49.713
Definitely. I believe so.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.870
Love my mom though.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.063
Look,
(laughing)

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.400
still love my mom.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:59.070
Even though she said
some mean (beep) to me,

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.263
you gotta forgive when you can.

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:06.303
- The most hurtful thing
that I've been told was,

00:01:08.122 --> 00:01:09.522
that I wasn't worth anything

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:12.630
by one of my parents.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.340
But being told that
you're not worth anything

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:18.210
by one of your parents.

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.590
I think that that messes you up mentally

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:23.740
and it sticks with you.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:29.990
- The most hurtful thing I've been told,

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.930
I was a nine year old and I
loved to play guitar and sing.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.960
And it was like my favorite thing,

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:43.230
I was like super passionate
about it, it was amazing.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.630
And I would listen to all my dad's music

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.400
and usually it was Cat Stevens.

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:48.600
And so I would just play the guitar

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:52.260
and then I started singing
like, moon shadow, moon shadow.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:01:55.390
And there was a woman, I heard
her walking up the stairs

00:01:56.910 --> 00:01:59.130
to, I don't know she was selling something

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.920
or was a church, you
know, like one of those

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:05.010
and she stopped and she was listening

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.570
and I could see her shadow.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.640
And so then she rang the doorbell.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:11.880
My dad came, he opened
the door and she's like

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.350
who has such a beautiful
voice, my goodness.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.230
So beautiful, she's amazing.

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:20.280
And he's like, yeah, yeah.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.840
Well, how can I help
you? What do you want?

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:22.690
Blah, blah, blah.

00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:30.210
And you know, I was awestruck
because of the first time

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:34.743
that somebody had actually acknowledged,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.013
I'm sorry.

00:02:38.013 --> 00:02:41.260
Acknowledged my voice and my singing

00:02:45.837 --> 00:02:48.660
and sorry it's been so
long and I'm still carrying

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.103
the hurt and the pain.

00:02:52.833 --> 00:02:54.660
But, when my dad came back in,

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:56.550
I said, wow, that was so cool.

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:59.430
Like she actually acknowledged

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:02.040
and said how beautiful my voice sounded.

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.370
He's like, no, your voice
sounds absolutely horrific.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:07.140
You should never sing.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.650
And so I've never sung since then,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.320
it kind of, I can't even
barely talk right now

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:14.853
'cause it's stifled my voice.

00:03:16.758 --> 00:03:19.070
So, so that's the most hurtful thing.

00:03:19.070 --> 00:03:22.325
- [Interviewee] So that
was the last time you sang?

00:03:22.325 --> 00:03:23.325
- Like that.

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:26.923
Yeah

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.900
- I do other stuff, but
singing is hard and I loved it.

00:03:33.540 --> 00:03:36.750
I thought I was gonna become
like a professional singer

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:39.750
(laughing) and I'm still
stuck at that moment.

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.263
And I don't know how to
like unstick from that.

00:03:44.764 --> 00:03:46.950
- The most hurtful thing
I've ever been told

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:49.860
was by a colleague who told
me right before I started

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:53.043
a new job, their advice to
me was, don't be yourself.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:57.540
And that I think they
were meaning to be honest,

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:01.161
in terms of like, you
know, don't be too bubbly,

00:04:01.161 --> 00:04:05.310
you wanna be professional but
it kind of crushed my soul

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:06.813
just a little bit, honestly.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:09.653
- I don't know.

00:04:09.653 --> 00:04:11.768
My mom used to tell me all the time

00:04:11.768 --> 00:04:12.780
that I'd end up like my dad,

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:15.510
not really amount to
anything, all the time.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.370
I don't think my dad's that bad.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:20.583
And it hurt me to think
that someone thinks like,

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:24.990
that my dad is less than what he is.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.790
And then thinking that I would amount to

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:29.553
less than what I am, you know.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:32.523
But, oh, well.

00:04:32.523 --> 00:04:35.520
- [Interviewee] Do you still
carry that hurt with you?

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.130
- No, pretty much just
left everything behind.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.920
I mean, I'm hear in (beep) San Diego

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.260
away from my hometown,
left everything behind.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:45.310
I don't even talk to most people anymore.

00:04:46.541 --> 00:04:48.543
So I'm over life, starting a new one.

00:04:50.195 --> 00:04:52.320
- I had my ex stepmother.

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:55.620
I'm a really talkative person,
I have been my whole life.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:59.460
And my ex stepmother once
told me that I should only say

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.830
every third thing that came to my mind

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.360
and that I should keep the rest to myself

00:05:03.360 --> 00:05:04.650
because she didn't wanna hear me talk.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:06.350
And it was something that I think,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.620
kept me from sharing a
lot of ideas and feelings

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.210
that I've had in my life.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:15.480
And in a lot of ways,
like kept me to myself

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:18.090
because I was thinking of only speaking

00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:19.440
every third thing out loud.

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:23.670
- I wouldn't say it's necessarily
the most hurtful thing

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:28.170
I've been told, but more
so the most painful things

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:33.093
that I wasn't told in childhood, you know,

00:05:34.769 --> 00:05:37.080
just like, honestly like,
I love you from my parents,

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:39.191
as dark as that is.

00:05:39.191 --> 00:05:40.200
- [Interviewee] Do you have siblings?

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.310
- Yeah. Older brother.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:43.350
- [Interviewee] Does he feel the same?

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:47.910
- Yeah. I mean, I still
remember like last year

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:49.440
when I hugged him for like the first time

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:51.990
in like so many years just
'cause we never like hugged

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:52.863
as a family.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:57.330
He like, looked at his shoes
sheepishly and he is like,

00:05:57.330 --> 00:05:59.730
wow I can't remember the
last time that we hugged.

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:00.900
And yeah, I don't know.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:04.590
Love just was never like a,
a thing that was like shown

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:05.670
or used in the family.

00:06:05.670 --> 00:06:08.280
So definitely it's more so what,

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.830
the things that hurt the
most was the absence of,

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:12.600
of words and acts and whatnot.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:15.393
Not so much hurtful things
directly being said.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.170
- In high school, I was at a pool party

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:23.700
and then after the party I
lost my virginity to this kid.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.250
And then the next day, oh my hair's curly,

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:28.740
so after we were swimming
and my hair was wet

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.070
curled up and then the day after
this kid took my virginity,

00:06:32.070 --> 00:06:33.240
tweeted about me and said,

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.980
I need a girl that looks good natural,

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:36.780
no Medusa looking ass.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:37.880
And that was about me.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.190
At the time, I was really upset.

00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:44.760
I straightened my hair every
day for like four years

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:46.830
and was extremely self-conscious

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:50.883
and thought about that
literally, I mean that was,

00:06:51.780 --> 00:06:54.360
like six years ago and I'm
still thinking about it.

00:06:54.360 --> 00:06:58.200
So yeah, it was really, hurt my feelings.

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.940
- My teacher in fifth grade told me

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:03.120
that I looked like an ad
for a depression commercial.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:04.830
Just out of nowhere, we were in class

00:07:04.830 --> 00:07:07.770
and he had me stand up in
front of the whole class

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:09.540
and was like, this is
what you look like, Sarah.

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:12.240
And started imitating
me because I have RBF.

00:07:12.240 --> 00:07:15.180
So if I'm not smiling,
I look angry I guess.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.060
And he started imitating me and
the whole class was laughing

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:20.340
and then he just kept
going for about 15 minutes,

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:21.840
until I was just left standing there

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.190
crying in front of everybody.

00:07:23.190 --> 00:07:25.160
It only ended because then
it was time for lunch.

00:07:25.160 --> 00:07:26.550
(laughing)

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.040
- [Interviewee] Do you still
carry that hurt with you?

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:33.030
- Yeah. I feel a lot of the
time where if I'm not smiling

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:34.830
or acting a certain way
then people won't wanna

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:35.663
be around me.

00:07:37.093 --> 00:07:40.860
- I've been told that I
shouldn't be in a relationship

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.130
just because I have
issues, like with myself,

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:47.613
but I go to therapy and
I work on myself anyway.

00:07:49.290 --> 00:07:52.020
I couldn't, (laughing)
I couldn't differentiate

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.910
whether I needed more therapy

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.560
or if I had to stop going to therapy

00:07:55.560 --> 00:08:00.120
'cause it was like, it was like
confirming that I was broken

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.983
and that I didn't deserve anything.

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:06.000
- That I'm, was fat, ugly

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.700
and didn't want to be married to me.

00:08:08.700 --> 00:08:11.308
No, actually it took me
longer to break up with him,

00:08:11.308 --> 00:08:13.110
20 years and six kids.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.200
It's a little more than
you could just walk away

00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:17.970
from something like that.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:21.630
I would say it's more,
still a damaging piece

00:08:21.630 --> 00:08:23.460
in the back of my mind.

00:08:23.460 --> 00:08:26.490
Yeah. I don't think anybody
really ever gets over that.

00:08:26.490 --> 00:08:29.220
- Most hurtful thing
is, no one's gonna want

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.570
to be your friend if you
keep acting like that.

00:08:32.940 --> 00:08:35.740
- No one will ever love
you if you are overweight

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:40.260
- If Chow loses weight,
she would look so pretty.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:42.060
- I don't wanna be with you anymore.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:45.720
- Oh, by the way, the two girls

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:48.840
that you witnessed their
birth, they're not yours.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.900
- I love you but I don't like you.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:52.410
- [Interviewee] Do you
remember who said it?

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:53.243
- My mom

00:08:54.120 --> 00:08:55.680
- [Interviewee] How did you get past it?

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:58.680
- A lot of drugs and then recovery.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:01.570
- Well, when I was a kid, I
heard my mom talking about me

00:09:02.730 --> 00:09:06.030
and my artwork and she said,
she'll never be an artist.

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:09.390
And I was like in kindergarten
or first grade or something.

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:12.960
And I still remember
that, obviously, so yeah.

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:14.550
And you know what, I'm not an artist.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:17.700
She was probably right
but it still was hurtful.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:21.000
I was surprised that she said that

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.950
because she was an artist.

00:09:22.950 --> 00:09:25.170
- [Interviewee] Do you carry
that pain with you at all?

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:26.003
Still today?

00:09:26.850 --> 00:09:29.550
- Only if somebody's talking
about art or drawing,

00:09:29.550 --> 00:09:32.850
not specifically art,
but just being artistic.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:34.800
I always say I'm not artistic at all.

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:37.140
It's probably 'cause my mom said that.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:38.573
- [Interviewee] You never
know who you might have been.

00:09:38.573 --> 00:09:40.173
- I could be artistic,

00:09:42.354 --> 00:09:44.670
but you know what, I do carry it, I guess,

00:09:44.670 --> 00:09:47.313
she said I wasn't, so I
still believe that I'm not.

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:52.320
- So in his defense it
was probably an accident,

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:56.520
but I, in high school,
my guidance counselor

00:09:56.520 --> 00:09:58.950
was talking to me about
why a lot of my friends

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:00.480
were in AP classes.

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:03.390
And I was really curious
why I was the only one

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:07.020
in my friend's group of
people, of similar coursework

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.970
who didn't get these
necessary, additional steps

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.010
in the right direction towards college

00:10:14.010 --> 00:10:16.500
and when I went to his office
and asked him about it.

00:10:16.500 --> 00:10:20.430
He just looked at me and said,
oh, you wanna go to college?

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:22.320
And it was like this moment of like,

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:24.870
well why, why wouldn't I?

00:10:24.870 --> 00:10:27.720
And like, it was like this real like,

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:32.490
oh is, is it I wonder if it's me,

00:10:32.490 --> 00:10:34.740
just like what he sees

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.680
and well, I'm working on my master's now

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:39.491
in computer science.

00:10:39.491 --> 00:10:40.950
So, I definitely wanted to go to college

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:45.870
but he definitely immediately
pulled out all the paperwork

00:10:45.870 --> 00:10:47.910
and stuff and was really
quick to talk about it.

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:51.030
But in that moment he just
like, the weight of like,

00:10:51.030 --> 00:10:55.140
oh I have to like say hi, yes,

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:58.620
I want to be involved in
academia, was like weird

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:00.600
because it was just like
something all my other friends

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.670
were just like recommended to.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:06.420
So that, that was, it wasn't
supposed to be hurtful

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:08.580
but man still to this day does it like,

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:11.233
I remember that conversation.

00:11:11.233 --> 00:11:14.550
- When I was in third grade
our my substitute teacher

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:18.090
asked me whether or not
I was in third grade

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:22.050
because I have dyslexia
and I couldn't read one

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:26.130
of the questions and I
asked her what it said.

00:11:26.130 --> 00:11:29.369
- [Interviewee] And do you
still carry that hurt with you?

00:11:29.369 --> 00:11:30.619
Does it still bother you?

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:35.600
- Kind of, and it's just a
little hard when you, you know

00:11:35.600 --> 00:11:38.043
I still am, I'm dyslexic and I can't,

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:41.460
it's hard for me to read, but
I'm better at reading now.

00:11:41.460 --> 00:11:42.930
- I love you.

00:11:42.930 --> 00:11:44.100
- [Interviewee] Can you tell us why?

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:46.445
- No, I'm not gonna tell you that.

00:11:46.445 --> 00:11:47.610
- [Interviewee] Okay.

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:50.130
- But it really spoiled that for me.

00:11:50.130 --> 00:11:52.830
When I hear, I love you,
I don't know whether

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.810
I really believe it now.

00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:55.960
It really (beep) me up.

00:11:57.540 --> 00:12:00.060
- The most hurtful thing was
when I was a little girl,

00:12:00.060 --> 00:12:01.350
growing up in Connecticut,

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:04.100
the neighborhood kids told
me to go back to my country.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.253
- [Interviewee] How'd that make you feel?

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:10.110
- Confused, really

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:10.943
Yeah.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:16.110
Yeah, mostly just confused,

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:17.760
like why would they say that to me?

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.960
I didn't understand like the
distance that they put me at.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:22.863
I didn't feel it.

00:12:24.420 --> 00:12:26.010
- [Interviewee] Do you still
carry that hurt with you,

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:26.843
do you think?

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:29.280
- Not really, not so much anymore.

00:12:29.280 --> 00:12:31.440
I've traveled so much
and the funny thing was

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:34.380
when I went back to my original country,

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:36.480
I didn't fit in there
either, I was American.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:40.110
So, I always felt like in
between worlds, kind of.

00:12:40.110 --> 00:12:44.010
But as an adult, I've taken
the best of all these worlds

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:46.803
and kind of integrated them into who I am.

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:49.590
- Well, the most hurtful
thing I've been told,

00:12:49.590 --> 00:12:51.270
there's been a few, but
the one that stuck with me

00:12:51.270 --> 00:12:52.800
and it still sticks with me,

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.470
is when I was two, I
was burned on an iron.

00:12:55.470 --> 00:12:57.630
My whole hand came off as a kid.

00:12:57.630 --> 00:12:59.970
So growing up as a kid
and people see my hand

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.790
they either say, I got, oh,
you have poop on your hand or,

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:04.710
oh, there's something wrong
with you, you're an alien.

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:08.910
And even, I'm, what? 38 now, 38 years old.

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:11.100
It still bothers me, I handicap it.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:12.540
I don't ever let people see it.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:15.873
And then I can show the camera, my hand.

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:19.980
So this, it still bothers me to this day.

00:13:19.980 --> 00:13:22.830
So I always talk like this,
this hand's bought up.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:23.670
I talk like this.

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:26.100
So nobody never sees it
'cause as soon as they do,

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:28.260
it's like, Ugh, it's
something that's disgusting

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:29.430
or it's poop.

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:31.350
And that's probably one
of the hurtful things

00:13:31.350 --> 00:13:33.510
I've ever been told because
it's still sticks with me

00:13:33.510 --> 00:13:36.270
at 38 years old, in my life.

00:13:36.270 --> 00:13:38.670
- [Interviewee] What do
you think can help you

00:13:38.670 --> 00:13:40.233
not be so hurt?

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:44.280
- To open up to it more and tell my story,

00:13:44.280 --> 00:13:46.800
'cause it's a story to tell,
it's history behind it.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:50.490
You know, so now sometimes
with friends, close friends

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:52.800
I'm able to actually deal
with it a little bit more

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.460
because I try to use
it as a positive thing

00:13:56.460 --> 00:13:58.320
and say, and I turn it like this and say,

00:13:58.320 --> 00:13:59.940
but if I turn it like this,
it look like it's a finger

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:00.947
pointing this way.

00:14:00.947 --> 00:14:03.510
And it does, if you look at it.

00:14:03.510 --> 00:14:04.680
So I try to do that.

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:06.540
So that's what I'm working on now.

00:14:06.540 --> 00:14:10.110
And I'm still working on that
at 38 years old, in my life.

00:14:10.110 --> 00:14:11.490
